<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime.

  Every key here weakens the hardened runtime, so the set is deliberately
  minimal and each entry has to earn its place by an observed failure rather
  than by precedent. The reference procedure (APPLE_NOTARIZATION.md §10.3)
  offers three plausible keys for a .NET app; only the ones below survived
  testing on this application. See the slice 5c report for what was tried.

  The failure mode this guards against is specific: the app notarizes and
  staples cleanly, then dies immediately on launch with a codesigning or
  memory-protection error. A missing entitlement is invisible until launch.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      The CoreCLR JIT writes native code into a memory page and then executes
      it. The hardened runtime forbids exactly that unless this is granted, so
      for any JIT-compiling runtime it is not optional.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
